  "patron": "France (secondary patron, named by Pius XI on 2 March 1922)",
  "relic_description": "<p>No bodily relics of Joan of Arc are known. After her death at Rouen on 30 May 1431, the English had her ashes and her heart thrown into the Seine so that no relics could be kept, as the bull of her canonization and the old French Lives record.</p><p>In 1867 a jar labeled “Restes trouvés sous le bûcher de Jeanne d’Arc, Pucelle d’Orléans” (remains found under the pyre of Joan of Arc, Maid of Orléans) was found in the attic of a Paris pharmacy. It held a charred-looking human rib, pieces of carbonized wood, a piece of linen about 15 cm long, and the thigh bone of a cat, and it was later kept in the museum at Chinon in the care of the Archdiocese of Tours. In 2007 a team led by Philippe Charlier examined it and concluded that the contents came from an Egyptian mummy and had not been burned. The Diocese of Tours said that the remains had never been considered relics by the Church and had never been venerated. The journal <em>Nature</em>, reporting the study, said they had been recognized by the Church; the two reports disagree.</p><p>At Orléans, relics kept in her memory, with a sword and banner, were destroyed during the Revolution in 1793. Her family’s house at Domrémy and the cross that the sentence of 1456 ordered raised at the place of her death in Rouen are monuments, not relics.</p>",
  "relic_location": null
 },
 "related": {
  "miracles": [
   {
    "miracles": "<p>This list gathers the healings, prophecies, and wonders attributed to Joan of Arc, with their sources and their status. Some come from Joan’s own answers at her trial in 1431, some from the sworn testimony of witnesses at the inquiry of 1450–1456, some from the bull of her canonization, and some from later pious tradition. It makes no claim of scientific proof, and it does not dismiss any account. Only the five cures in the first section were examined and approved by the Church. Every other entry is a report, and its status line says who made it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"approved-by-the-church\">Miracles approved by the Church</h2>\n<p>For Joan’s beatification four cures were proposed, and Pius X approved three of them in a decree of the third Sunday of Advent 1908; the fourth was dispensed. Two more cures were approved for her canonization in a decree of 6 April 1918. The bull of 1920 names the people healed and their illnesses, and calls each cure instant and perfect. It does not give the dates or places of the cures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m01\">Sister Thérèse of Saint Augustine: a chronic stomach ulceration</h3><p>Sister Thérèse of Saint Augustine was healed instantly and perfectly of a chronic ulceration of the stomach (<em>chronica ulceratione stomachi</em>), according to the bull of 1920. A modern fan site describes her illness as leg ulcers; the bull’s diagnosis is used here.</p><p>Status: approved by the Church for the beatification (decree of 1908).</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m02\">Sister Julie Gauthier of Saint Norbert: an ulcer of the breast</h3><p>Sister Julie Gauthier of Saint Norbert was healed instantly and perfectly of a painful fungoid ulcer of the left breast (<em>ulcere fungoso eretistico in sinu sinistro</em>), according to the bull of 1920.</p><p>Status: approved by the Church for the beatification (decree of 1908).</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m03\">Sister Jeanne Marie Sagnier: tuberculosis of the bone</h3><p>Sister Jeanne Marie Sagnier was healed instantly and perfectly of a chronic tuberculous osteoperiostitis, a disease of the bone and its lining (<em>osteoperiostite chronica tuberculari</em>), according to the bull of 1920.</p><p>Status: approved by the Church for the beatification (decree of 1908).</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m04\">Maria Antonia Mirandelle: a perforating disease of the foot</h3><p>Maria Antonia Mirandelle was healed instantly and perfectly of a perforating disease of the sole of the foot (<em>morbo plantari perforante</em>), according to the bull of 1920.</p><p>Status: approved by the Church for the canonization (decree of 6 April 1918).</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m05\">Thérèse Belin: tuberculosis and a damaged heart valve</h3><p>Thérèse Belin was healed instantly and perfectly of tuberculosis of the peritoneum and the lungs, and of an organic lesion of the mitral valve of the heart (<em>tuberculosi peritoneali ac pulmonali necnon laesione organica orificii mitralici</em>), according to the bull of 1920.</p><p>Status: approved by the Church for the canonization (decree of 6 April 1918).</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<p>The bull adds that the processes describe many other graces, spiritual and bodily, and wonderful healings, but it does not list them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"during-joans-life\">During Joan’s life</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m06\">A defeat announced at Vaucouleurs, February 1429</h3><p>While she was still at Vaucouleurs, Joan announced that the French had just been defeated near Orléans, in the fight later called the Battle of the Herrings. The news arrived days later and confirmed it. The <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> reports the episode.</p><p>Status: historical claim of prophecy, reported by the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> (1910).</p><p>Source: Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m07\">Eleven days through enemy country, February 1429</h3><p>Joan rode from Vaucouleurs to Chinon in eleven days, crossing many rivers and country held by the English and Burgundians. The bull of 1920 says the king heard from Baudricourt that she had come through <em>prodigiose</em>, like a wonder, and that he then agreed to receive her.</p><p>Status: stated in the papal bull of 1920; the eleven days are also in Guérin’s Life.</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 394.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m08\">The king recognized among his courtiers, Chinon, March 1429</h3><p>When Joan was brought before Charles at Chinon, the old French Lives say she went straight to him among his courtiers. He told her he was not the Dauphin, and she told him that he was. At her trial she said she knew him “by the counsel of my Voice.” The bull of 1920 says only that she revealed to him secrets known to God and the king alone.</p><p>Status: traditional narrative, accepted by the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>; Joan’s own words at trial.</p><p>Source: Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 395; T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), p. 13, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m09\">The secret sign given to the king, Chinon, 1429</h3><p>Joan gave Charles a private sign that convinced him. The <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> says what it was is unknown, and that the most common belief is that it touched a doubt about his birth. At her trial in 1431 Joan first refused to tell it (“You will not get that from me this year”) and later described it in the form of an allegory of an angel bringing the king a crown.</p><p>Status: unresolved; Joan’s own account at trial.</p><p>Source: Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>. Also T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), pp. 59–72, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 1, p. 75, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam01joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m10\">The sword with five crosses, Fierbois, April 1429</h3><p>Joan’s voices told her that a sword lay in the ground at the church of Saint Catherine at Fierbois. She wrote to the churchmen there, and they found it, rusty, with five crosses on it, and sent it to her. She told her judges she never saw the man who went to dig. Guérin adds that it lay behind the altar, and that she told the king of it only after much urging.</p><p>Status: stated by Joan at her trial and in the papal bull of 1920.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 1, pp. 75–76, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam01joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 396.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m11\">Predictions recorded in a letter from Lyon, 22 April 1429</h3><p>A letter written at Lyon by the Sire de Rotslaer on 22 April 1429 records what Joan was then saying she would do: raise the siege of Orléans, be wounded by a shaft without dying of it, and see the king crowned at Reims that summer. The <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> says the letter was registered at Brussels before the events, and cites the original manuscript.</p><p>Status: documented prophecy, reported by the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>.</p><p>Source: Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m12\">Her wound at Orléans foretold</h3><p>Guérin’s Life says Joan told Charles before Orléans that she would be wounded there and not hindered, and that on the morning of 7 May she announced she would be wounded that day.</p><p>Status: told in a nineteenth-century Life; compare the Lyon letter above.</p><p>Source: Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, pp. 396, 398.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m13\">The wind on the Loire, April 1429</h3><p>When Joan met Dunois on the bank of the Loire, the wind was against the boats carrying food for Orléans. Dunois testified under oath in 1456 that the wind changed at once, “as if in a moment,” as she spoke, so that the sails were raised and the boats passed the English bastille of Saint-Loup. A second Orléans witness agreed about the wind. Dunois said that this, among other things, made him judge her deeds to be from God rather than from man.</p><p>Status: sworn testimony at the inquiry of 1456; not investigated as a miracle.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, pp. 5–7, 17–18, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m14\">The arrow wound at Les Tourelles, 7 May 1429</h3><p>During the assault on the earthwork at the bridge of Orléans, an arrow struck Joan between the neck and the shoulder and went in half a foot deep. Dunois testified that she did not stop fighting and took no medicine. Guérin’s Life says she pulled the arrow out herself and was comforted by a vision of two saints. The <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> says she was wounded in the breast. Dunois’s Latin deposition, as printed, dates the wound to the twenty-seventh of May, which is a slip for 7 May.</p><p>Status: historical fact with a reported vision; witness testimony and pious tradition.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, p. 8, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 398; Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m15\">The English withdraw without a fight, 8 May 1429</h3><p>On the morning after the earthwork fell, the English came out of their camps in order. Dunois testified that Joan rose from her bed, armed in a coat of mail only, and forbade any attack: let them go. They marched away and the siege ended. Guérin presents their retreat as made at her word.</p><p>Status: historical event; the reading of it as a wonder is Guérin’s.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, p. 9, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 398.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m16\">A warning at Jargeau, June 1429</h3><p>During the assault on Jargeau, Joan told the duke of Alençon to move from the place where he stood, or a machine would kill him. Soon afterward a lord named du Lude was killed on that spot. D’Alençon told the story under oath in 1456.</p><p>Status: sworn testimony at the inquiry of 1456.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, pp. 96–97, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m17\">A stone on her helmet at Jargeau, June 1429</h3><p>As Joan climbed a ladder with her banner at Jargeau, a stone struck her helmet and she fell into the ditch. She rose at once and cried, “Friends, friends, up! up! Our Lord has condemned the English.” The town was taken.</p><p>Status: sworn testimony of the duke of Alençon; told also by Guérin.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, p. 97, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 400.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m18\">Verses of a Maid from the Bois-Chenu, 1429</h3><p>Dunois testified that when the English commander Suffolk was a prisoner after Jargeau, a paper reached him with four lines of verse saying that a Maid would come from the Bois-Chenu and ride on the backs of the archers. A prophecy of a maiden from the Bois-Chenu was known in the countryside.</p><p>Status: sworn testimony of Dunois; popular prophecy.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, p. 15, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m19\">Her horse stood still at the town cross, 1429</h3><p>A letter written by the Laval brothers in June 1429 tells that Joan’s horse stood still at the town cross.</p><p>Status: popular account in a contemporary letter; cited in a note by Murray.</p><p>Source: T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), p. 30, note 1, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m20\">“A year and not much more,” 1429</h3><p>The duke of Alençon testified that Joan told the king she would last “a year and not much more,” and named four tasks for that year: drive out the English, crown the king at Reims, free the duke of Orléans, and raise the siege of Orléans. She was captured at Compiègne in May 1430, a little over a year after Orléans. Guérin ties the prophecy to her capture.</p><p>Status: prophecy in sworn testimony; fulfilled, as her supporters saw it, by her capture.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 3, p. 99, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam03joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, pp. 399, 402.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m21\">A dead newborn at Lagny, 1429–1430</h3><p>Joan told her judges that at Lagny a baby three days old, who had died without baptism, was brought before the statue of the Virgin. The child was black, like her tunic. Joan and the girls of the town prayed, and the child gasped three times and the color began to return. He was baptized, then died and was buried in consecrated ground. The bull of 1920 says she raised a dead infant to life so that he could be baptized.</p><p>Status: told by Joan at her trial; described in the papal bull as a raising from the dead.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 1, about p. 105, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam01joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m22\">Her capture foretold at Melun, April 1430</h3><p>On the ramparts of Melun, Joan’s voices told her that she would be taken prisoner before the feast of Saint John. She was captured at Compiègne on 23 May 1430 (some sources say 24 May).</p><p>Status: Joan’s own account; repeated in the bull, Guérin, and the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>.</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 402; Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m23\">Compiègne freed before Martinmas, 1430</h3><p>After her capture a steward of her captors told Joan that Compiègne would fall, and she said it would not. The bull of 1920 says her voices had told her the town would be freed before the feast of Saint Martin, and that it was.</p><p>Status: prophecy stated in the papal bull; Joan’s reply reported by the chronicler Perceval de Cagny.</p><p>Source: Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 4, p. 35, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam04joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m24\">Comfort in her prison, Rouen, 1431</h3><p>Joan told her judges that on 24 February 1431 her voice woke her in her prison and told her, “Answer boldly; God will help thee.” Guérin’s Life says her voices and her saints visited and comforted her throughout her imprisonment.</p><p>Status: Joan’s own words at trial; pious tradition in Guérin.</p><p>Source: T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), pp. 16–17, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 404.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m25\">A greater prize than Orléans, 1 March 1431</h3><p>At her trial on 1 March 1431, Joan said that within seven years the English would lose a bigger prize than Orléans. The English lost Paris on 12 November 1437.</p><p>Status: prophecy recorded at the trial; reported as fulfilled by the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>.</p><p>Source: Herbert Thurston, “St. Joan of Arc,” <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, vol. 8 (1910), <a href=\"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08409c.htm\">New Advent</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m26\">The voices after her abjuration, May 1431</h3><p>After she signed the abjuration at Saint-Ouen on 24 May 1431, Joan said her voices rebuked her for giving in and told her she had damned herself to save her life. The bull of 1920 says that at the end she asked for martyrdom, and that her voices told her not to be anxious or troubled about it, for she would enter the kingdom of Paradise.</p><p>Status: Joan’s own words at trial; repeated in the papal bull.</p><p>Source: T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), p. 137, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m27\">Birds that ate from her lap</h3><p>The hostile <em>Journal</em> of a citizen of Paris repeats a story that birds came to eat from Joan’s lap, but gives it only as hearsay, with the Latin note <em>in veritate apocryphum est</em>, “in truth it is apocryphal.”</p><p>Status: legend; doubted by the source that records it.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 4, p. 463, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam04joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"at-her-death-and-after\">At her death and after</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m28\">The name of Jesus, Rouen, 30 May 1431</h3><p>Witnesses at the inquiry said Joan called on Jesus again and again in the flames, more than six times by one account, and said the name once more as she bowed her head at the last. The bull of 1920 and Guérin add that many saw the name of Jesus written in the flames.</p><p>Status: her cries are in sworn testimony; the name seen in the flames is reported in the bull and Guérin.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 2, pp. 6–7, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam02joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), p. 301, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 412.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m29\">A white dove from the fire, Rouen, 30 May 1431</h3><p>Isambart de la Pierre reported that an English soldier saw Joan’s spirit leave her, “as it were a white dove, going away from France.” The bull and Guérin say the soldier fainted and thought he saw a white dove. The soldier is not named.</p><p>Status: reported by a witness at the inquiry; popular.</p><p>Source: T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), p. 191, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 412.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m30\">The soldier with the bundle of wood, Rouen, 30 May 1431</h3><p>An English soldier had sworn to throw a bundle of wood on Joan’s fire. Isambart de la Pierre said the man was stunned when he heard her cry the name of Jesus, and later confessed.</p><p>Status: reported by a witness at the inquiry, years after the event.</p><p>Source: T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), pp. 162, 191, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m31\">The heart that would not burn, Rouen, 30 May 1431</h3><p>After the execution the executioner came in terror to the friars Isambart de la Pierre and Ladvenu. He told them that despite oil, sulphur, and charcoal he could not burn Joan’s heart and entrails to ashes, and called it “a most evident miracle.” He said he feared he was damned. Guérin adds that the heart was found whole and full of blood. On the orders of the English, the ashes and the heart were thrown into the Seine.</p><p>Status: reported by two friars at the inquiry of 1450; stated in the papal bull.</p><p>Source: Jules Quicherat, ed., <em>Procès de condamnation et de réhabilitation de Jeanne d’Arc</em>, vol. 2, pp. 7, 9, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/procsdecondam02joanuoft\">Internet Archive</a>; T. Douglas Murray, trans., <em>Jeanne d’Arc, Maid of Orleans</em> (1902), pp. 161–162, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/jeannedarcmaidof00murruoft\">Internet Archive</a>; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>. Also Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, p. 412.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m32\">The fate of her judges</h3><p>Guérin’s Life lists the deaths of those who condemned Joan as a kind of judgment: Cauchon dying suddenly in a barber’s hands, Le Maistre vanishing, Estivet found dead in a ditch, L’Oiseleur dying suddenly at Basel, and Midi struck with leprosy, together with the fates of Bedford and Henry VI. The bull of 1920 says only that the guilty died shamefully.</p><p>Status: popular tradition in a pious source; treat as legend.</p><p>Source: Paul Guérin, <em>Les Petits Bollandistes</em>, vol. 15, 30 May, “Jeanne d’Arc, la Pucelle d’Orléans”, pp. 413–414; Benedict XV, bull <em>Divina disponente</em>, 16 May 1920, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xv/la/bulls/documents/hf_ben-xv_bulls_19200516_divina-disponente.html\">vatican.va</a>.</p>",
    "time_period": [
     "medieval_period",
     "modern_era"
    ]
   }
  ],
  "teachings": [
   {
    "teachings": "<p>Joan of Arc wrote no books, and she could not read. What she left is the example of her life and the words she spoke: her answers to her judges at Rouen in 1431, a few letters she dictated, and sayings her companions remembered under oath twenty-five years later. The themes below describe that example. They are not a system of doctrine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"listening-and-answering-boldly\">Listening, and answering boldly</h2>\n<p>For Joan, faith began with listening. She believed God spoke to her through her voices nearly every day, and she treated what she heard as an order to be obeyed, whatever it cost.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“I was sitting on the bed; I joined my hands; I implored its help. The Voice said to me: ‘Answer boldly; God will help thee.’”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc to her judges, Rouen, 24 February 1431</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She told this to her judges on the morning it happened, on a day when she would face her judges again, in a prison guarded by English soldiers. She lived it from the start: when Baudricourt sent her home from Vaucouleurs, she came back until he received her; and in court she warned the bishop judging her that he put himself in great danger.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-and-god-will-work\">Work, and God will work</h2>\n<p>Joan did not see trust in God as a reason to wait. God acts, she believed, and so must we; the timing belongs to him.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“Do not doubt; the hour is ready when it pleases God. … Work, and God will work.”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc to the duke of Alençon at Jargeau, June 1429, as he testified in 1456 (our translation)</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She said it when d’Alençon thought the assault on Jargeau premature. At Orléans she lived it the other way round: when the army was exhausted and Dunois wanted to withdraw, she rode alone into a vineyard, prayed for a quarter of an hour, and then planted her banner on the edge of the ditch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-grace-of-god\">The grace of God</h2>\n<p>Joan never claimed to be sure of her own holiness. She asked God to give and keep his grace, and left the rest to him.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“If I am not, may God place me there; if I am, may God so keep me.”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc, asked whether she was in the grace of God, Rouen, 24 February 1431</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>The court asked her the question at her trial. She lived the answer in frequent confession: her hostess in Bourges saw her confess often, and at her first hearing she refused to say the Our Father unless the bishop would hear her confession.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mercy-in-the-middle-of-war\">Mercy in the middle of war</h2>\n<p>Joan led soldiers, but she would not kill, and she could not bear to see blood spilled, on either side.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“Better, forty times better, my banner than my sword!”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc to her judges, Rouen, 1431</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She told her judges she carried the banner “to save killing any one, for I have never killed any one.” Her page saw her get down from her horse to hold the head of a dying English prisoner while he made his confession, and on the morning the English left Orléans she forbade any attack on them. Asked whether her saints hated the English, she answered, “They love what God loves: they hate what God hates.”</p>\n<h2 id=\"falling-and-rising-again\">Falling and rising again</h2>\n<p>Joan’s life includes two falls, and she named both honestly. She jumped from the tower of Beaurevoir against the counsel of her saints, and she signed an abjuration at Saint-Ouen out of fear.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“All I said and revoked, I said for fear of the fire.”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc to her judges, Rouen, 28 May 1431</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She said it four days after the abjuration, knowing that taking it back would cost her life. After the leap from Beaurevoir she confessed and asked God’s pardon, and later she called it the gravest fault of her life.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prayer-and-the-mass\">Prayer and the Mass</h2>\n<p>Prayer and the sacraments were the frame of Joan’s days, from the village church of Domrémy to her prison cell.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“I would rather die than be in irons! but if I am allowed to go to Mass, and am taken out of irons and put into a gracious prison … I will be good, and do as the Church wills.”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc to her judges, Rouen, 28 May 1431</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>As a girl she knelt in the fields when the church bell rang and scolded the sexton when he forgot to ring for Compline. At Blois she told the soldiers to go to confession and received Communion with them, and at Bourges she asked to go to Matins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"simplicity-before-the-learned\">Simplicity before the learned</h2>\n<p>Joan could not read, and she was not ashamed of it. She believed that what God taught was more than any scholar knew.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“There is more in our Lord’s books than in yours.”</p><p><cite>— Joan of Arc, on the clerics who examined her, as Marguerite La Touroulde testified in 1456 (our translation)</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She told the theologians at Poitiers, “I know neither A nor B.” When the women of Bourges brought her their rosaries to touch, she laughed and told them that their own touch would do as much good as hers.</p>",
    "time_period": [
     "medieval_period"
    ]
   }
  ],
  "quotes": [
