 "gaps": [
  "TO CHECK (dates the caller flagged): birth date; Reims coronation 17 July 1429; trial dates (21, 22, 24, 27 Feb., 1 Mar., 24, 28, 29 May 1431); beatification 18 Apr. 1909; canonization 16 May 1920. All appear in the notes but were not verified against a primary page in this session.",
  "Birth: 6 Jan. 1412 (Boulainvilliers, via Murray’s note and the Catholic Encyclopedia, ‘probably’) vs 6 Feb. 1412 (bull of 1920). birth_year left null under the Directus rule for disputed dates; the biography says only ‘about nineteen’ in 1431, her own words.",
  "Dunois’s deposition, as the OCR reads it, dates the arrow wound ‘vicesima septima die maii’ (27 May). The biography uses 7 May (Guérin, Catholic Encyclopedia). Check the Latin page (Quicherat III p. 8).",
  "Capture date: 23 May 1430 (Guérin, Bourgeois, Cagny) vs 24 May (bull, Catholic Encyclopedia). The biography uses 23 May; the source note mentions the conflict.",
  "Perceval de Cagny is used for scenes only (Blois, Paris, Crépy, Compiègne), never for dates.",
  "Murray’s page numbers come from OCR running heads and are approximate. Murray’s English was not compared with Quicherat’s Latin and French for the trial answers.",
  "‘They love what God loves: they hate what God hates’: page in Murray to check (about p. 86).",
  "Patay saying (‘if they were hanging from the clouds we would have them’): OCR damage in Quicherat III pp. 98–99; check the scan.",
  "Witness quotations translated from OCR text of Quicherat; check each against the page scan before print (notes-witnesses §0).",
  "The Chinon words (‘I am called Joan the Maid…’) and the ‘not the Dauphin’ exchange come from Guérin’s nineteenth-century Life and are attributed to ‘the old French Lives’ in the text.",
  "Chinon jar: Nature (2007) says the remains were recognized by the Church; the Diocese of Tours (EWTN 2007) says they never were. Both are stated in relic_description. The date of the mummy is not given because the full paper was not read.",
  "The letter to the Hussites (3 March 1430) is doubtful (survives only in German; composed by a clerk, per Quicherat) and is not quoted.",
  "Death-scene words come from depositions of 1449–1456, and the biography labels them as testimony. Toutmouillé’s deposition is dated 1449 in the notes; Isambart and Ladvenu swore on ‘5 March 1449’ (Easter-style year), i.e. March 1450 by modern count (researcher’s inference).",
  "The research paragraph gives no count of witnesses because the notes give none; ‘more than twenty details’ is a count of NEW tags across the three notes files.",
  "Approved cures: the bull gives names and illnesses but no dates or places; a fan site disagrees on the diagnoses. The bull is followed.",
  "Feast rank: the notes say the 1960s reform removed her from the General Roman Calendar but she is kept locally, especially in France; the exact rank is not known. feast_day_catholic is set to 30 May from the bull.",
  "Isabelle Romée’s words at Notre-Dame in 1455 (‘I demand that her name be restored’) come only from a fan site and are not quoted.",
  "No prayer saved; no verified image.",
  "read_next: michael-the-archangel and catherine-of-alexandria are not on the site; the identification of Catherine is an assumption."
 ],
